lever-moments-ask = When is a lever in balance?
lever-moments-title = The condition of a lever’s balance
lever-moments-question = A lever ruler hangs on an axis in a stand and balances by itself; the holes for the hooks are every 5 cm. We hang 100 g weights left and right at different distances from the axis, then balance them with a dynamometer. Each time we find the moment on each side: M = F·l. When is a lever in balance?
lever-moments-choices = When the moments on both sides are equal: F₁·l₁ = F₂·l₂
lever-moments-choices-2 = When the forces on both sides are the same
lever-moments-choices-3 = When the arms on both sides are the same
lever-moments-answer-why = Imagine the lever turned a little about its axis. Every point of it moves along an arc, and the farther a point from the axis, the longer its arc: at 20 cm twice as long as at 10 cm (the path s = l · angle). The lever itself adds no energy and takes none (there is little friction at the axis), so the work done by the force on one side equals the work on the other: F₁·s₁ = F₂·s₂. Put s = l · angle — the angle is the same for the whole lever and cancels: F₁·l₁ = F₂·l₂. That is why what matters is neither the force alone nor the arm alone but their product — the moment. This is the “golden rule of mechanics”: half the force — but twice the path; what you gain in force you lose in distance. That is why a door handle sits far from the hinges, a nut is undone with a long spanner, and a crowbar lifts a stone hands cannot move.
lever-moments-answer-say = Equal moments — and the lever balances!
lever-moments-text-list = and the dynamometer { $value }
lever-moments-text-list-2 = and { $F2 }
lever-moments-text-list-3 = { $F1 } · { $l1 } cm { $value } · { $l2 } cm: M₁ ≈ { $F12 } N·m, M₂ ≈ { $F2 } N·m
lever-moments-answer-text = { $list }. The forces on the two sides differed, so did the arms, and every time the lever stood level: the moments were equal. The condition of a lever’s balance: F₁·l₁ = F₂·l₂ — as many times smaller a force, as many times longer its arm. This is the law of the lever, known since Archimedes. Differences in the hundredths come from the dynamometer reading only tenths of a newton.
